%I A402197 #14 Nov 15 2026 10:33:09
%S A402197 11,13,491,8387,8389,2182513,2182561,2182567,2182573,2182811,2182813,
%T A402197 2183593,2183681,2183737,2183749,2184283,2184293,2184323,2184361,
%U A402197 2184389,2184397,2184407,2184473,2184499,2184503,2184509,2184779,2184799,2184827,2184913,2184971,2185009
%N A402197 Primes p for which pi_{24;17,19}(p) - pi_{24;1,11}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A402197 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,11} and S = {17,19}, corresponding to T = {chi_12, chi_{-24}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A402197 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_12(q) + chi_{-24}(q))/2 = 1.
%C A402197 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;17,19}(x) > pi_{24;1,11}(x) has logarithmic density 0.97244, making the race between {1,11} and {17,19} the 20th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24" below.
%C A402197 Let D(x) = pi_{24;17,19}(x) - pi_{24;1,11}(x). Below x0 = 8*10^12 there are respectively 34876145777, 34876274835, 34876248066, 34876268167, 34876282644, 34876258940, 34876298151, and 34876294229 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 143147. There are 10 sign-changing zones giving a total of 799032 terms below x0:
%C A402197   (1) a(1..2) = 11-13;
%C A402197   (2) a(3) = 491;
%C A402197   (3) a(4..5) = 8387-8389;
%C A402197   (4) a(6..92) = 2182513-2192887 (there are 265 primes a(6) <= p <= a(92) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -9 at p = 2190371);
%C A402197   (5) a(93..282) = 48175201-48321017 (there are 3839 primes a(93) <= p <= a(282) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -28 at p = 48231889);
%C A402197   (6) a(283..1253) = 66545641-69511171 (there are 37464 primes a(283) <= p <= a(1253) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -53 at p = 69313091);
%C A402197   (7) a(1254..21373) = 1144047913-1387955893 (there are 5656854 primes a(1254) <= p <= a(21373) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -558 at p = 1372565531);
%C A402197   (8) a(21374..50269) = 41000147963-45363706667 (there are 129730014 primes a(21374) <= p <= a(50269) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -5974 at p = 43657624441);
%C A402197   (9) a(50270..106844) = 48682933811-51611429003 (there are 23652201 primes a(50270) <= p <= a(106844) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1081 at p = 50300393291);
%C A402197   (10) a(106845..799032) = 751249462753-1018608926009 (there are 2621131722 primes a(106845) <= p <= a(799032) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -11005 at p = 902357057579).
%C A402197 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 2780212364.
%H A402197 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402197/b402197.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..50269</a> (the first 8 sign-changing zones)
%H A402197 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402197 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402193/a402193_1.txt">Sign-changing zones of five prime number races modulo 24</a>.
%H A402197 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24159887">Sign-changing zones of prime number races between cosets modulo 24, and of pi(x;8,5) - pi(x;8,1), up to 7*10^12</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402197 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402197/a402197.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..400000</a>, <a href="/A402197/a402197_1.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 400001..799032</a> (all terms below 8*10^12).
%H A402197 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A402197 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A402197 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A402197 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A402197 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==11, k--); if(p%24==17||p%24==19, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A402197 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A402197 nonn,new
%O A402197 1,1
%A A402197 _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_, Nov 15 2026
